when the supreme court overturned roe  versus wade it did more than just allow  states to ban abortion  it showed how this court views rights  not explicitly listed in the  constitution it's really difficult to  exaggerate the importance of this  decision the court has pulled a complete  180. going from recognizing a  constitutional right and individual  right to saying 50 years later no  we are mistaken  here's the legal reasoning behind that  180 and why even though the court's  opinion made clear it applies solely to  abortion some legal experts think the  same methodology could be used to revise  other rulings  the framers wrote the bill of rights the  amendments to the constitution to well  list the rights americans have  the right to free speech to bear arms to  not have soldiers in your house during  peace time you know them and at some  point specifically at the ninth one they  wrapped it up by saying this list isn't  exhaustive and there are other rights  not named here that cannot be denied or  disparaged or in 18th century  terminology the rate's not in the  enumeration so the ninth amendment is  there essentially to say that there are  unenumerated rights right that the list  um in the bill of rights uh at the time  um was not intended to be exclusive uh  and so then the difficult part becomes  figuring out what those unenumerated  rates are the supreme court has long  seen the right to privacy as one it  established that right in cases  involving raising children that parents  have the right to teach them another  language or send them to private school  and in cases involving having children  that the government could not sterilize  convicted criminals  and in the landmark 1965 case griswold  vs connecticut which was based on these  earlier rulings that overturned a  connecticut ban on contraception  in the opinion the supreme court ruled  that marital privacy even though not  mentioned explicitly in the constitution  is one of those unenumerated rights  protected by the ninth amendment  and that right meant using birth control  was a private decision and couldn't be  banned by the state  there was a lot of different kind of  ideas of privacy that flowed from  griswold cases that expanded the right  to birth control the right to same-sex  relations and eventually marriage were  based on these cases that established  privacy as an unenumerated right that  was sort of a constellation of privacy  interests that arose in that way  the right to an abortion was one of  those that was established based on  these rulings the phrase in in roe v  wade it says that the right to privacy  that was identified in griswold is broad  enough to encompass the decision to  end an unwanted pregnancy  the row opinion mentioned that right  could also be found in another amendment  the 14th which says no one shall be  deprived of liberty without due process  it says we believe that's based on the  liberty clause  of the 14th amendment or the due process  clause another name for that clause  roh is from tradition that looks at  these concepts of liberty  and equality very broadly okay it  applies them very broadly to new  circumstances and to  a society that is different than the  society that we had in the 18th century  but two of the justices thought that was  too broad of an interpretation the  dissenters in 1973 said that the  constitution is silent on this matter it  doesn't say anything about abortion one  way or the other and this court has no  business interfering with the  legislature's judgment about how to  regulate a pregnancy  and that's pretty much the reasoning  that the court has adopted now  in overturning roe the court wrote that  an unenumerated right should be deeply  rooted in this nation's history and  tradition  and ruled that the constitution does not  confer a right to abortion the  methodology is called originalism  where you stick very closely to what you  think  the  authors of the passage  understood it to mean what the public  understood the passage to mean  at the time it was adopted but some  legal experts and civil rights activists  are concerned about how this methodology  could be applied to some of these other  rulings involving privacy like in  griswold or in obergefell versus hodges  which legalized same-sex marriage in  2015 but the majority wrote that those  cases do not destroy potential life like  abortion they even wrote nothing in this  opinion should be understood to cast  doubt on precedents that do not concern  abortion  but the dissenters argued that even if  the majority's word is trusted  that law has a way of evolving without  regard to original intentions and  whatever today's majority might say one  thing really does lead to another this  broader approach to applying the liberty  concepts and equality concepts of the  constitution the current majority of the  supreme court is very skeptical of that  and that doesn't mean that they are  eager necessarily to overrule those  precedents or different conditions and  different facts that apply to each of  them  but as a matter of principle yes those  would be on on shaky grounds the idea  that they would be overruled wholesale  the way that roe versus wade has been  overruled  a little harder to imagine because of  the social acceptance is broader the  court is certainly saying this is just  about abortion but there are kind of  there's a breadcrumb trail the court is  leaving in the future if it wants to go  further than abortion and i don't think  we can rule that out um indefinitely  experts like ziegler say there's no  immediate prospect that these other  rulings would be reversed but the  supreme court views unenumerated rights  much differently than previous courts  and that may play out in future cases
 

